    "question": "What is the root cause of StaleElementReferenceException in Selenium? How do you resolve it properly, and why does Playwright not suffer from this issue?",
    "expectedFocus": "DOM re-rendering / AJAX replacing nodes, WebElement reference pointing to detached node, solving with dynamic polling or re-finding element, Playwright locators are lazy evaluators with automatic re-query.",
    "answerSummary": "### 1. Conceptual Breakdown (Plain English)\n**What it is:** StaleElementReferenceException occurs when an element was located, but the DOM updated (AJAX, re-render, React virtual DOM reconciliation) and the element was destroyed and replaced.\n**Why it matters in QA:** Understanding this concept enables QA engineers to bridge the gap between business specifications and technical verification, eliminating guesswork and test flakiness.\n\n### 2. Deep-Dive Technical Explanation\nStaleElementReferenceException occurs when an element was located, but the DOM updated (AJAX, re-render, React virtual DOM reconciliation) and the element was destroyed and replaced. In Selenium, resolve by re-finding the element inside a retry loop or using FluentWait. In Playwright, locators do not store static DOM node pointers; they re-query the DOM automatically before every action, eliminating stale element exceptions by design.\n\n### 3. Step-by-Step Practical Application Guide\n• **Step 1: Identify Preconditions & Scope:** Clarify the exact system boundary, inputs, and expected environmental states before executing.\n• **Step 2: Construct Deterministic Assertions:** Avoid relying on arbitrary delays; instead, assert against explicit system state transitions and verifiable outcomes.\n• **Step 3: Isolate Environmental Variables:** Ensure test data is cleanly provisioned and cleaned up so parallel or subsequent runs remain independent.\n\n### 4. Key Terminology for QA Engineers\n• **Deterministic Testing:** A testing approach where a given input always produces the exact same observable outcome without intermittent or random failures.\n• **Flakiness (Flaky Test):** A test that intermittently passes and fails on identical application builds without any code changes, usually caused by timing race conditions or shared mutable state.\n• **Quality Gate:** An automated checkpoint in the CI/CD pipeline that blocks flawed builds from progressing to staging or production.",
    "codeSnippet": "// Selenium StaleElement Retry Handler:\npublic void clickWithRetry(By locator) {\n  new WebDriverWait(driver, Duration.ofSeconds(10))\n    .ignoring(StaleElementReferenceException.class)\n    .until(d -> { d.findElement(locator).click(); return true; });\n}",
    "bestPractice": "Use lazy locators and avoid storing WebElements as class member variables.",
    "pitfallToAvoid": "Catching Exception with empty catch blocks to hide stale element failures.",
    "technology": "Playwright"
